<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moors</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moor, Mauri, Moros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moor is a European exonym chiefly for the Muslim populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The Moors were not a single or self-defined people: Europeans applied the name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, Muslim Europeans and black peoples, and the word, with its racial connotations, has fallen out of fashion among scholars since the mid-20th century. It goes back through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauri">Mauri</a> to the Berbers of Mauretania and was applied to the inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> after Muslim Berbers and Arabs conquered the Iberian Peninsula in 711. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1492, its Muslims were known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> until their expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1609.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moors.jpg" alt="Moors" width="330" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfonso X (Life time: 1221-1284) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MoorsinIberia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…eastern Pyrenees between the County of Foix to the north and Catalonia to the south, appear among the parishes of the diocese of Urgell in a document traditionally dated to 839, and a later legend credited Charlemagne with granting the Andorrans their freedom for help against the <b>Moors</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Afonso de Albuquerque said that taking Malacca from the <b>Moors</b> would ruin Cairo and Mecca and leave Venice no spices but what her merchants bought in Portugal; Tomé Pires wrote that &quot;whoever is lord of Malacca shall have his hands on the throat of Venice&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Castanhoso reported that &quot;the <b>Moors</b>&quot;, as he called the invaders, had tried and failed to destroy the churches with crowbars and gunpowder.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>With crusader help, Afonso I, the first king of Portugal, ended a siege begun on 1 July and took Lisbon from the <b>Moors</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-25">25 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating the <b>Moors</b> of the taifa of Balansiya, King James I of Aragon entered the city of Valencia, an event regarded as the dawn of the Kingdom of Valencia, a component realm of the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
